  "thesis_summary": {
    "thesis": "Cisco Systems (NASDAQ: CSCO) sells networking hardware, security software, collaboration tools, and observability platforms to enterprises, service providers, and governments worldwide. **[Fact]** FY2025 consolidated revenue was **$56.7 billion** with three reportable geographic segments (Americas, EMEA, APJC). **[Fact]** The company generates **$14.2 billion** of cash from operations and returns most of it through dividends and buybacks while maintaining a capital-light model (capital spending roughly **6%** of operating cash flow). **[Inference]** After the Splunk acquisition, a growing share of revenue is recurring software and subscription, but the equity still behaves like a mature infrastructure compounder priced for AI-era networking growth.",
    "why_market_wrong": "The market may be paying for AI networking, security cross-sell, and Splunk subscription mix while our proof uses **screening-grade** reinvestment bounds that are not yet stock-specific. **[Inference]** At **$110.70**, the quote sits near the contract high-case intrinsic value (**$111.25**) but **91% above** the base present value (**$58.05**), so the inefficiency is not a hidden discount but a **growth-and-quality premium** that our automated template does not underwrite. **[Inference]** Predictive attribute: **none** for a dated payoff; return is earnings power and franchise quality at price until reinvestment assumptions are refreshed from primary-source capital bridges.",
    "stance": "watch",
    "archetype": "compounder",
